    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=199260cf-4b15-410c-9970-1d94a408c2d5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201480930590_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Enrollment Management",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\n\nLocation: Laurel\n\nJob Description – Senior Director of Enrollment Management   Institutional Overview Capitol Technology University is a career-focused university located in Laurel, Maryland, between Baltimore and Washington, D.C. It is a fully-accredited non-profit private university that was started in 1927 by a Navy veteran to teach students how to build radios. The University offers unique commitments to on-campus students that include a salary guarantee and a Chargers Assistance Program that helps students pay back their loans if they do not find full-time work within 90 days of graduation. Position Summary Reporting to the Vice President for Academic Affairs and Enrollment and working in close collaboration with the Senior Director of Student Recruitment, the Senior Director of Enrollment Management serves as the strategic and operational leader of undergraduate and graduate enrollment management. The Senior Director of Enrollment Management holds leadership over enrollment strategy, application review, enrollment outcomes, and the performance and culture of the Admissions team. The Senior Director partners closely with other directors to advance shared goals, and collaborates with the VPAAE in driving strategic enrollment growth strategies. The Senior Director will be a highly motivated individual with strong management, organizational, and interpersonal skills, who is passionate about growing enrollment and ensuring a smooth admissions pathway for prospective students. This is a full-time exempt position on our beautiful 52-acre campus in Laurel, MD, on the Baltimore Washington parkway. Responsibilities Lead admissions operations including the following areas: Admissions operational workflows Application review and admissions policy Data, technology, and CRM (JRM, Salesforce) Transfer and pathway partnerships Budget and resources Develop and execute comprehensive, data-driven strategic enrollment management plan to increase enrollment and attract a diverse applicant pool; Oversee the evaluation of applications, ensure consistent and holistic reviews, and finalize acceptance or rejection decisions in coordination with academics; Recruit, develop, and retain a business-minded and service-oriented admissions team, cultivating a culture of collaboration, accountability, and excellence; Track and analyze enrollment metrics and application trends to report directly to the VPAAE; Design and supervise targeted communication plans to guide prospective students through the application process and yield admitted students; Perform other duties as required. Qualifications Master’s degree in a related field or an equivalent combination of education and experience; Possess at least 10 years of experience in leading and developing teams; Demonstrated experience building and executing student enrollment strategies; Possess proficiency with CRM and SIS (Jenzabar and Salesforce is preferred); Exhibit an ability to perform administrative duties with speed and accuracy; Possess the ability to develop successful partnerships with internal and external stakeholders; Possess excellent management skills; Must be able to work in the U.S. without sponsorship.   Physical Demands There are limited physical demands; however, this position requires sitting occasionally for extended periods of time, and repetitive motions for tasks such as operating a computer mouse and keyboard, and hearing and speaking on the telephone. The candidate must be able to lift, pull, bend, grasp, and occasionally lift up to 20 lbs.   Salary Range The salary for this position typically ranges from $95,000 to $105,000 annually, depending on experience and qualifications.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=199260cf-4b15-410c-9970-1d94a408c2d5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201480705247_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Enrollment Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\n\nLocation: Laurel\n\nJob Description – Undergraduate Enrollment Advisor   Institutional Overview Capitol Technology University is a career-focused university located in Laurel, Maryland, between Baltimore and Washington, D.C. It is a fully-accredited non-profit private university that was started in 1927 by a Navy veteran to teach students how to build radios. The University offers unique commitments to on-campus students that include a salary guarantee and a Chargers Assistance Program that helps students pay back their loans if they do not find full-time work within 90 days of graduation. Position Summary Reporting to the Senior Director of Student Recruitment, the Undergraduate Enrollment Advisor coordinates student enrollment efforts by recruiting and guiding prospective and incoming students through the application and admission process. The UEA will represent the university at recruitment events such as high school visits, college fairs, and campus tours. The UEA will also be expected to actively recruit new students within assigned territories through email, phone, and personal outreach. The UEA will be a highly motivated individual with strong organizational and interpersonal skills, who is passionate about growing enrollment and ensuring a smooth admissions pathway for prospective students. This is a full-time exempt position on our beautiful 52-acre campus in Laurel, MD, on the Baltimore Washington parkway. Responsibilities Maintain the recruitment of new student inquiries within assigned territories Communicate with prospective students through multiple communication channels including in person meetings, CRM, email, text messaging and phone conversations Establish and maintain strategic outreach activities and initiatives Assist with enrollment operations and special events Present information and represent Capitol Technology University at virtual, on-and-off campus events Effectively communicate and enthusiastically sell the value and story of the university and its programs to a diverse population of prospective students Maintain up to date knowledge on all programs and application processes Utilize CRM (Salesforce) to track communications, summarize meetings, plan and host events and send targeted email and text messages with inquiries, applicants and admitted students Review applications for acceptance into programs, develop admissions folders, ensure all acceptance requirements are met and included in files, and make scholarship recommendations Frequent evening and weekend work is expected during the fall and spring recruitment cycles (travel, night, and weekend work required) Perform other duties as required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in a related field or an equivalent combination of education and experience; Must be able to travel within the designated area and attend in-person meetings. 3 years of related experience is preferred; Familiarity with CRM and SIS (Jenzabar and Salesforce is preferred); Familiarity with MS Office suite; Possess excellent interpersonal and business development skills; Must have valid driver’s license and a reliable fully insured vehicle capable of handling movements within assigned territory; Must be able to work in the U.S. without sponsorship.   Physical Demands The candidate must be able to stand for up to 4 hours, and lift, pull, bend, grasp, and occasionally lift up to 25 lbs.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=199260cf-4b15-410c-9970-1d94a408c2d5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201479724362_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Facilities Maintenance Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\n\nLocation: Laurel\n\nPosition Title : Facilities Maintenance Technician Reports To : Director of Facilities Location : Capitol Technology University, Laurel, Maryland Position Type : Full-Time Position Overview Capitol Technology University seeks a hands-on Facilities Maintenance Technician to join our Facilities team. This position is responsible for helping maintain, repair, improve, and renovate the University’s buildings, grounds, furnishings, and related systems. The successful candidate will have a practical general construction or facilities maintenance background and be comfortable performing a wide range of routine repairs and small renovation projects involving carpentry, drywall, painting, plumbing, electrical, mechanical, and finish work.    The position is not intended to require a licensed specialist in every trade. Rather, the University is seeking a versatile maintenance professional who can independently complete work typically associated with an experienced maintenance technician or early-career journeyman, recognize when specialized or licensed contractor support is required, and assist the Director of Facilities in keeping campus facilities safe, functional, attractive, and well maintained. Candidates with advanced skills in one or more construction trades are especially desirable. Key Responsibilities General Maintenance, Repairs & Troubleshooting Perform routine and intermediate-level repairs to University buildings, classrooms, offices, laboratories, residence areas, common spaces, and other campus facilities. Diagnose building maintenance issues, identify practical repair solutions, obtain needed materials, and complete work in a safe and timely manner. Perform general carpentry and finish work, including framing and blocking, door and hardware repair, trim and molding, shelving, cabinetry, ceiling tile replacement, and other wood or composite material repairs. Repair and finish drywall and plaster surfaces, including patching, sanding, preparation, painting, caulking, and other interior and exterior finish work. Perform basic plumbing work such as repairing leaks, unclogging drains, replacing faucets, valves, toilets, supply lines, traps, and other fixtures, and identifying issues that require a licensed plumber. Perform basic electrical maintenance permitted for the position, such as replacing switches, receptacles, lighting fixtures, lamps, ballasts/drivers, and similar components, while referring work requiring a licensed electrician to the appropriate contractor. Assist with mechanical and HVAC maintenance, including filter replacement, basic troubleshooting, thermostat and equipment checks, minor component replacement where appropriate, and coordination with service vendors for more complex repairs. Maintain, assemble, repair, relocate, and install University furniture, fixtures, equipment, appliances, signage, and building accessories as needed. Renovation & Campus Improvement Projects Complete small renovation and improvement projects from demolition through final finish, including selective demolition, wall repair or construction, drywall installation, painting, flooring and ceiling repairs, fixture installation, trim work, and related punch-list items. Assist the Director of Facilities with planning and sequencing campus renovation projects, estimating material needs, preparing work areas, coordinating access, and minimizing disruption to University operations. Read and follow basic plans, sketches, manufacturer instructions, and project scopes in order to complete repair and renovation work accurately. Coordinate with outside contractors and vendors as directed, including providing site access, explaining field conditions, monitoring progress, and assisting with project closeout or corrective work. Identify opportunities for cost-effective repairs and improvements that extend the useful life of campus buildings, finishes, fixtures, and equipment. Preventive Maintenance & Facility Inspections Conduct routine inspections of campus buildings, rooms, roofs and accessible exterior areas, doors and hardware, plumbing fixtures, lighting, finishes, equipment, and other facility components to identify maintenance needs before they become larger problems. Perform and document preventive maintenance on building systems, vehicles, tools, equipment, and facility components in accordance with established schedules and manufacturer recommendations. Respond promptly to work orders and service requests, document completed work, and communicate unresolved or higher-risk conditions to the Director of Facilities. Maintain organized inventories of commonly used maintenance supplies, replacement parts, tools, and materials and notify the Director when replenishment is needed. Grounds, Exterior & Seasonal Maintenance Perform or assist with landscaping and groundskeeping activities, including mowing, trimming, planting, mulching, debris removal, and general seasonal upkeep. Inspect and maintain exterior building elements, walkways, site furnishings, signs, fences, drainage areas, and other campus exterior features within the capabilities of the position. Participate in snow and ice removal, storm preparation and cleanup, and other weather-related campus response activities. Help maintain the cleanliness, appearance, and overall curb appeal of University buildings and grounds. Safety, Tools & Operational Support Use hand tools, power tools, ladders, lifts, utility vehicles, grounds equipment, and other maintenance equipment safely and properly. Maintain clean and orderly work areas and follow applicable workplace safety practices, University procedures, and OSHA-related requirements. Assist with campus event setup and breakdown, including tables, chairs, staging, room configuration, and related facility support. Provide after-hours, weekend, and emergency facilities support as scheduled, including participation in the University’s on-call rotation. Perform other facilities-related duties and projects as assigned by the Director of Facilities. Required Qualifications Four (4) or more years of progressively responsible experience in facilities maintenance, general construction, building repair, or a related skilled trade. Equivalent combinations of directly relevant experience and technical training will be considered. Demonstrated ability to independently complete routine and intermediate maintenance, repair, and small renovation projects using sound construction and repair practices. Working knowledge of several building trades, such as carpentry, drywall/plaster, painting, plumbing, basic electrical, mechanical/HVAC maintenance, flooring, ceiling systems, or related construction disciplines. Proficiency in the safe use of common hand tools, power tools, ladders, maintenance equipment, and groundskeeping equipment. Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills, including the ability to determine whether work can be completed internally or should be referred to a licensed or specialized contractor. Ability to plan and organize assigned work, identify required materials, complete tasks with limited supervision, and communicate status or concerns to the Director of Facilities. Ability to interact professionally with students, faculty, staff, contractors, and visitors while working in occupied campus environments. Valid driver’s license and dependable transportation. Ability to meet the physical requirements of the position and successfully complete the University’s pre-employment screening requirements. Physical Requirements  The ability to bend, stretch, twist, or reach with your body, arms, and/or legs. Must be able to push, pull, lift, carry, or maneuver weights up to fifty (50) pounds independently and one hundred (100) pounds with assistance. Ability to physically access exterior and interior parts of the building, climb a ladder, and access small crawlspaces. Requires eye-hand coordination and manual dexterity sufficient to operate tools and equipment. Sensory capability to detect odors and hear sounds necessary to detect, diagnose, and repair equipment. Periodic exposure to outside elements where temperature, weather, odors, and/or landscape may be unpleasant and/or hazardous.  Work Schedule Full-time, 40 hours per week with flexibility for evenings, weekends, and shift adjustments. Participation in an on-call rotation for after-hours emergency response. Compensation & Benefits Competitive salary commensurate with experience.  Comprehensive benefits package including health, dental, vision, retirement plan, and paid time off.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=199260cf-4b15-410c-9970-1d94a408c2d5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201472487508_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Dir. Transfer and Adult Admissions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\n\nLocation: Laurel\n\nCAPITOL TECHNOLOGY UNIVERSITY Position Description: Assistant Director, Transfer & Adult Admissions  Full-time 12-month, Exempt Position About Capitol Technology University Capitol Technology University is the only independent university in Maryland dedicated to engineering, computer science, information technology and management of technology. Founded in 1927, Capitol Technology University is a regionally accredited institution offering associate, bachelor's, master's and doctorate degrees, as well as professional development training and certificates. The university's 52-acre campus is located in Laurel, Maryland, a suburban setting midway between Washington, DC and Baltimore. Capitol Technology University blends academic excellence with practical learning experiences that prepare students for a range of challenging and competitive careers. Job Summary Reporting to the Director of Undergraduate Admissions & Recruitment, the Assistant Director of Transfer & Adult Admissions will coordinate with principal partner leads at various regional, and designated extra-regional two-year institutions to maintain relationships and schedule off site engagement events to connect with prospective students, establish faculty advocacy, and drive applications into our articulated programs. Engage with regional military base Education Services Officers (ESOs) to explore recruitment and outreach opportunities for military personnel and their dependents. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Oversee the recruitment of prospective transfer and adult, and military students within assigned territories, utilizing various communication channels including in-person meetings, CRM, email, text messaging, and phone conversations. Collaborate with faculty to establish and maintain articulation agreements. Identify and cultivate partnerships and articulation opportunities with external institutions, coordinating discussions with relevant stakeholders. Develop and execute a comprehensive transfer recruitment and communications plan, setting clear enrollment targets. Lead the organization and execution of on-campus and virtual undergraduate recruitment events. Collaborate with internal departments for event logistics and communication. Transfer Credit Evaluation: Coordinate alongside the Records and Registration Office, the evaluation of incoming transcripts, professional certifications, CLEP, DANTES, and Joint Service Transcripts for adult, military, and transfer students. Establish an evaluation methodology to measure the effectiveness of events and report results to team members and the Director of Undergraduate Admissions & Recruitment. Travel, night, and weekend work is required. POSITION QUALIFICATIONS: Must have valid driver’s license, reliable vehicle, and be able to travel. Strong relationship management skills Experience in meeting or exceeding enrollment targets Excellent communication skills across various channels Familiarity with Microsoft Word, Excel, Access and Outlook Flexibility, dedication, initiative, confidentiality and the ability to learn. Bachelor’s degree required; master’s preferred 2-3 years of professional or related experience in higher education or sales preferred",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=199260cf-4b15-410c-9970-1d94a408c2d5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201472086613_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Research Methods Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\n\nLocation: Laurel\n\nResearch Methods Faculty Job Description Institutional Overview Capitol Technology University is a career-focused university located in Laurel, Maryland, between Baltimore and Washington, D.C. It is a fully-accredited non-profit private university that was started in 1927 by a Navy veteran to teach students how to build radios. The University offers unique commitments to on-campus students that include a salary guarantee and a Chargers Assistance Program that helps students pay back their loans if they do not find full-time work within 90 days of graduation. Position Summary The successful candidate will be passionate about working directly with Master’s and Doctoral students to prepare them for the demands of their Master’s thesis or Doctoral dissertation research and assist dissertation chairs in mentoring their students on implementing rigorous academic research studies. The individual will be a dynamic, visionary educator with innovative ideas for interdisciplinary approaches to education, experience teaching in multiple modalities, and the ability to motivate student excellence. Faculty dedicate 60% of their time to teaching and 40% of their time to service. Teaching includes course preparation, instruction, thesis/dissertation chairing, learning assessment, and participation in course and curriculum review. Service includes co-curricular activities which enhance and enrich the student experience and/or contribute to new student recruitment, including oversight of research courses, resources, and processes. Service also includes membership on the Academic Review Board and Institutional Review Board. Faculty may participate in open houses, participate in outreach (Cyber Saturdays, STEM Challenge, Capitol Connect, etc.), direct individualized contact with prospective students, and other engagement activities. Applicants with traditional and non-traditional backgrounds in research with additional experience in any field relevant to the Graduate School academic programs are welcome to apply. This is a full-time exempt on-campus position on our beautiful 52-acre campus in Laurel, MD, on the Baltimore Washington parkway. Responsibilities: Teaching Instruct students in the research-related courses in the Master’s and Doctoral programs. Including: qualitative and/or quantitative methodologies, thesis/dissertation processes, empirical research design, and APA style formatting of the thesis/dissertation. Assigned to oversee mentored methodological courses within the thesis/dissertation process in the Master’s and Doctoral programs. Maintain high visibility for research students, including but not limited to; regular office hours, responding to students' emails and voice mails in a timely fashion, supporting co-curricular activities, supporting enrollment events (Open Houses, STEM Challenge, etc.…), serving on committees, mentoring and advising students on academic and publication development. Manage classrooms in person, via Zoom, and within the LMS including preparing syllabi, assignments, activities, grading, preparing assessment activities, etc. Respond to student emails within 2 business days of receipt. Respond to student submissions within 5 business days with substantive feedback. Maintain professional competencies in research, a subject area provided in the Graduate School, and currency in instructional methodologies through professional associations and professional development. Ability to teach in a multimodal environment, on campus, simultaneous instruction (streaming via Zoom while teaching in a classroom) and Live Online (streaming via Zoom) and use LMS (Canvas). Interest in developing understanding of and participation in asynchronous instruction. Support student success initiatives by making timely referrals to the advising team. Effectively use Office365, Canvas (Learn@), Zoom (Capitol Live), and Student Information System interfaces. Responsibilities: Service Support educational effectiveness activities, such as course learning assessment and program level assessment review. Serve as a permanent member of the Academic Review Board (ARB) and/or the Institutional Review Board (IRB). Support accreditation activities of the university. Provide mentorship to dissertation chairs on research techniques and tools needed to ensure rigorous student research studies. Support enriching co-curricular student experiences including the advising and mentoring of students, participating in activities aligned with the curriculum, such as guest speakers, student research, communicating with students within specified degree programs regularly, etc. Support the recruitment of new Master’s and Doctoral students including: participating in Open House activities, assisting in the development of relationships and articulation agreements with research partners, developing and maintaining a professional image on social media (including LinkedIn, etc.), developing relationships with appropriate professional organizations, participating in special events (on campus or virtual) focused on research, creating and sharing content online (blogs, webinars, video clips, etc.) focused on research and subject areas within the Graduate School. Perform other tasks as assigned by the Vice President for Academic Affairs. Qualifications Doctoral degree from an accredited institution of higher learning in a closely aligned field relevant to the subject areas provided within the Graduate School. Must be able to work in the U.S. without sponsorship as the college is unable to provide sponsorship. Extensive knowledge of subject matter and relevant topics and issues in the research discipline, including, but not limited to, qualitative and/or quantitative methodologies, statistical testing, data analysis techniques, understanding of relevant analysis software, and in-depth understanding of the ethical principles of research. This can be demonstrated through at least five years of documented research activities and relevant research publications within the last 10 years. Knowledge of effective instructional techniques and innovative modes of course delivery adaptable to a variety of learning styles. Understanding of the current American Psychological Association (APA) formatting guidelines. Teaching experience of five years or more, to include at least two years teaching and/or chairing dissertations at the doctoral level. Excellent communication skills, and the ability to work with faculty, students, and industry partners on various projects. Ability to master the use of the technology platforms used to support learning and administration of the University including: Office365, Canvas (Learn@), Zoom (Capitol Live), and Student Information System interfaces.   Salary Range The salary for this position typically ranges from $85,000 to $95,000 annually, depending on experience and qualifications.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=199260cf-4b15-410c-9970-1d94a408c2d5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201472083518_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Administrative Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\n\nLocation: Laurel\n\nAdministrative Faculty Job Description Institutional Overview Capitol Technology University is a career-focused university located in Laurel, Maryland, between Baltimore and Washington, D.C. It is a fully-accredited non-profit private university that was started in 1927 by a Navy veteran to teach students how to build radios. The University offers unique commitments to on-campus students that include a salary guarantee and a Chargers Assistance Program that helps students pay back their loans if they do not find full-time work within 90 days of graduation. Position Summary The Administrative Faculty will provide support in administration, recruitment, teaching and service related to all of academics but with a focus on the undergraduate programs. This position will be responsible for ensuring quality for administrative and operational tasks, marketing and growing the programs, promoting an on-campus undergraduate student experience, and managing the quality and integrity of the programs. The successful candidate must embrace a hands-on approach, possess a business-mindset, and thrive in a collaborative environment. Accordingly, Capitol strongly encourages industry professionals with an appropriate Master’s degree and teaching experience to apply for this position. This is a full-time on-campus 12-month appointment on our beautiful 52-acre campus in Laurel, MD, on the Baltimore Washington parkway. Responsibilities Work effectively with all stakeholders to grow enrollment for the University in alignment with the mission and vision Support and execute academic administrative and operational tasks (e.g., management of student workers, lab/class schedules, pre/post award grant management, etc.) with a focus on the undergraduate programs Instruct students in a STEM discipline (engineering, computer science/cybersecurity). Including: designing and executing learning activities that stimulate student involvement and encourage critical thinking, performing accurate assessment of student performance, providing timely constructive feedback to support student knowledge advancement Maintain high campus visibility including but not limited to; regular office hours, timely responses to student communications, support for co-curricular activities, enrollment events (Open Houses, STEM Challenge, Accepted Student Day, Capitol Connect, community college and high school visits, etc.), committee service, and academic and career mentoring. Promote the University by engaging strategic external opportunities Communicate effectively with all constituents including students, faculty, staff, parents, and alumni Collaborate with government and industry partners to provide opportunities for our students Demonstrate successful leadership in education and professional service Support accreditation and assessment efforts for the Department Perform other duties as required Qualifications Master’s or PhD from an accredited institution of higher learning in a relevant STEM field 5+ years of combined experience in administrative and/or teaching experience as well as professional experience in a related field Must be able to work in the U.S. without sponsorship Industry experience is a plus and those working full-time in industry are strongly encouraged to apply   Salary Range The salary for this position typically ranges from $85,000 to $95,000 annually, depending on experience and qualifications.",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
